  "grafo_evidencia": {
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8a8c7aec-23fe-5d4a-8bd6-064fd7611559",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Rhodesia, Northern Rhodesia, Southern Rhodesia, Zambia, Zimbabwe, Cape Town, the Zambezi\n- People: Cecil Rhodes\n- Companies: British South Africa Company\n- Organizations: British government\n- Topics: British imperialism, colonial administration, place naming, zoo history\n\nFacts:\n- In 1895, Cecil Rhodes and the British South Africa Company founded the southern African territory of Rhodesia, covering what are now Zimbabwe and Zambia, and the company named it after Rhodes.\n- In 1924, the British government began administering Northern Rhodesia as a protectorate under conditions similar to other British-administered protectorates, ending the special provisions used under British South Africa Company rule.\n- In 1931, Groote Schuur Zoo was established in Cape Town at the request of the deceased Cecil Rhodes.\n- The territory north of the Zambezi was officially designated Northern Rhodesia by the British South Africa Company and became Zambia in 1964.\n- Groote Schuur Zoo was a free and very popular attraction in Cape Town until it closed sometime between 1975 and 1985.\n- The territory south of the Zambezi was dubbed Southern Rhodesia by the British South Africa Company and became Zimbabwe in 1980.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "20083811-8649-5f5c-8f51-ddd847335b03",
        "text": "Page: Rhodesia (region). Sentence: The territory to the north of the Zambezi was officially designated Northern Rhodesia by the Company, and has been Zambia since 1964; that to the south, which the Company dubbed Southern Rhodesia, became Zimbabwe in 1980.\nPage: Northern Rhodesia. Sentence: From 1924 it was administered by the British government as a protectorate under similar conditions to other British-administered protectorates, and the special provisions required when it was administered by BSAC were terminated.\nPage: Cecil Rhodes. Sentence: An ardent believer in British imperialism, Rhodes and his British South Africa Company founded the southern African territory of Rhodesia (now Zimbabwe and Zambia), which the company named after him in 1895.\nPage: Groote Schuur Zoo. Sentence: Established in 1931 at the request of deceased Cecil Rhodes, it was free of charge and a very popular attraction in Cape Town until its closure sometime between 1975 and 1985.",
